#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Route one TCP front door to many ephemeral sessions on filesystem sockets.

This is the component the reverse proxy talks to. It is the only thing on the
box listening on TCP, and it is the only leg where a credential does any work:
below it every session is reached through /run/hdw4s-proxy/<instance>.sock,
where who may connect is already decided by the socket's mode and its
directory's permissions, before any HTTP exchange happens.

WHY THIS PARSES EVERY REQUEST INSTEAD OF SPLICING, and it is not a preference.
The cheap shape -- read the first request's headers, pick a backend, then become
a byte pipe -- is unsound. HTTP/1.1 pools connections per ORIGIN, so two
sessions on one hostname under different paths are the same origin, and request
2 is delivered to request 1's backend. That is a cross-session read, not a
cosmetic mis-render. It was measured with two stand-in backends and a
one-connection-per-request control: the control routed both correctly and the
pooled case mis-routed, so the failure is the pooling and not a broken rig.
Pooling defeats SPLICING, not path identity -- anything that parses every
request sees the path on every request.

THE FALSE GREEN THIS INVITES, because it will catch whoever tests this next: a
stand-in reverse proxy at its DEFAULTS opens a fresh upstream connection per
request and does not reproduce the defect at all. A test that does not
explicitly enable upstream keep-alive passes while the defect is live.

The price is availability rather than correctness: one component whose failure
takes every session, where the per-session socket-proxyd it replaces took
exactly one. That is why the listening socket stays in a socket unit -- a crash
loses in-flight connections and systemd hands the next arrival to a restarted
router, instead of the front door latching off.

THE THREE CARRIERS, and none of them is a capability.

  cookie hdw4s_id   per browser (and per storage partition, so a private window
                    is a separate identity for free)  -- IDENTITY: what this
                    browser may own. Minted here. NEVER derived from a request
                    header: no vhost in this estate strips a client's identity
                    header, so trusting one would be an auth bypass, and it
                    would also foreclose sharing with browsers the reverse proxy
                    never vetted.
  path /s/<sid>/    per tab, because a tab has one URL -- SELECTION: which of my
                    sessions this tab is showing. An ADDRESS, not a capability:
                    a session's own uid can read its own environment, so any
                    path we hand downstream is in somebody else's access log by
                    construction. Authorization happens here, before any byte
                    reaches a session.
  sessionStorage    per tab -- arrival state, read by the page, never load
                    bearing for access.

OWNERSHIP KEYS ON THE SESSION INSTANCE, NEVER ON THE SLOT. Sharing is additive
and never revoked, so the ownership table only grows and nothing collects it.
Meanwhile a slot is reaped for idleness and its uid and socket are RECYCLED. An
entry keyed on the slot would therefore route a browser that returns after its
session was reaped into whatever now occupies that slot -- a stranger's desktop,
handed over by the ownership check meant to prevent exactly that. The sid is
minted per session and never reused; the slot name is a value in the record, not
its key. assert_ownership_keying() below exists to be seen failing.
"""

